; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Mandelbrot in Dots
; CATEGORY: Fractals
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     Mandelbrot set, complex numbers, WHILE, DOT, symmetry
; SUMMARY:  Draws a chunky, retro Mandelbrot set one dot at a time, using its mirror symmetry to save work.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   For every dot on a grid the program asks: if I start at zero and
;   keep squaring and adding this point, does the number stay small
;   or fly away? Points that stay small are in the Mandelbrot set
;   and are painted black. The others get a color that says how
;   quickly they escaped. Since the set is the same above and below
;   the middle line, each answer paints two dots.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run and wait a few seconds. Make :grid smaller for a finer
;   picture (it gets slower), or bigger for an even chunkier one.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * complex numbers as a pair of ordinary numbers
;   * WHILE with two conditions joined by AND
;   * mapping screen coordinates to math coordinates
;   * using symmetry to halve the work
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Raise :limit to 60. The edge gets sharper but it is slower.
;   * Change the palette list to your own colors.
;   * Zoom in: change :zoom to 600 and :centerx to -0.75, then look
;     at the "seahorse valley" near x = -0.75, y = 0.1.
; ============================================================

; ESCAPE outputs how many rounds it takes for z = z * z + c to leave
; the circle of radius 2, or :limit if it never does.
; c is (cx, cy). z is (x, y); squaring it gives (x*x - y*y, 2*x*y).
to escape :cx :cy
  localmake "x 0
  localmake "y 0
  localmake "n 0
  while [and :n < :limit (:x * :x + :y * :y) < 4] [
    localmake "newx :x * :x - :y * :y + :cx
    make "y 2 * :x * :y + :cy
    make "x :newx
    make "n :n + 1
  ]
  output :n
end

; SHADE picks a color for a count: black inside the set, and a color
; that cycles through the palette for points outside.
to shade :n
  if :n = :limit [output [0 0 0]]
  output item 1 + remainder :n count :palette :palette
end

; PLOT works through the top half of the screen, one row at a time.
; The screen point (px, py) is the complex number
;   (px / zoom + centerx, py / zoom).
to plot
  for [py 0 300 :grid] [
    for [px -400 400 :grid] [
      setpencolor shade escape :px / :zoom + :centerx :py / :zoom
      dot list :px :py
      dot list :px minus :py
    ]
  ]
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground 0
make "grid 5
make "limit 30
make "zoom 210
make "centerx -0.6
make "palette [[20 30 110] [40 70 180] [70 130 230] [130 190 250]
               [240 240 200] [250 200 90] [230 130 40] [170 60 40]]
; A pen a little wider than the grid leaves no gaps between dots.
setpensize :grid + 1
plot
